---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/best-usb-size-for-djing#article',
  headline: 'What Size USB Do DJs Actually Need?',
  description: 'How many tracks fit on a DJ USB, why bigger isn\'t safer on CDJs, and the size that professionals actually carry.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-24',
  dateModified: '2026-07-27',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  // ARTICLE IMAGE (2026-08-04). Google's Article guidance asks for an image,
  // and without one an Article node is never a candidate for a rich result.
  // This is the article's own share card, generated from its own title by
  // scripts/gen-og.mjs, so it represents this article and nothing else.
  // mainEntityOfPage is an @id reference rather than a bare URL string so it
  // resolves to the WebPage node Base.astro emits, instead of being a URL
  // that merely looks like one.
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/best-usb-size-for-djing#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/og/best-usb-size-for-djing.png',
};
---
<Base
  title="What Size USB for DJing? 32GB vs 64GB vs 128GB | Save My Gig"
  description="How many tracks fit on a DJ USB, why bigger isn't safer on CDJs, and the size that professionals actually carry."
  path="/fix/best-usb-size-for-djing"
  crumbName="USB size"
  ogImage="/og/best-usb-size-for-djing.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line">Drive_Size</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">HOW BIG SHOULD A<br>DJ <span class="accent">USB</span> BE?</h1>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>Short answer: 32-64GB, quality brand, and buy two.</strong> That's several thousand tracks, more music than you could play in a week of sets, with fast mounting and full FAT32 comfort.</p>
    </div>

        <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The track math</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>MP3 320kbps:</strong> ~9MB per track → a 32GB drive holds ~3,500 tracks; 64GB ~7,000.</li>
      <li><strong>Lossless (AIFF/WAV):</strong> ~40-60MB per track → 32GB ≈ 600 tracks, 64GB ≈ 1,200, still 20+ hours of music.</li>
      <li>A 4-hour set is 60-80 tracks. Even the lossless 32GB carries eight full sets of options.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">Why huge drives are worse in booths</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>Mount time:</strong> players index the database on insert, a stuffed 256GB drive can leave you staring at a loading screen mid-changeover.</li>
      <li><strong>Browsing:</strong> 20,000 tracks on a CDJ screen is not a library, it's a haystack. Curation beats capacity.</li>
      <li><strong>Failure blast radius:</strong> when the everything-drive dies, everything dies. Two medium drives beat one giant one - <a href="/fix/dj-usb-backup-strategy">the two-drive strategy →</a></li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">What to actually buy</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>32 or 64GB from a major brand, metal body preferred (booth drinks happen), USB-A: it works on every player. USB-C storage ports appear only on the CDJ-3000X and CDJ-1500X.</li>
      <li>Format <span class="mono" translate="no">FAT32 + MBR</span> regardless of what it ships with - <a href="/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj">here's why</a>.</li>
      <li>Two identical ones. Always two. <a href="/gear">The exact models we trust →</a></li>
    </ul>
      </div>
    </details>



    <ArticleCTA article="usb_size" />
  </main>
</Base>
